; MusicTrail.inix -- how this database opens, and how each table is heard.
;
; ONE HUNDRED ALBUMS AND TWO HUNDRED MUSICIANS, from two books by Jamal Mazrui.
; Mastery in Music Genre: Among the Greatest Albums of All Time gives the albums,
; ten in each of ten genres in ranked order, each with its artist, year, summary
; and awards. Mastery in Popular Song: Among the Greatest Musicians of All Time
; gives the musicians, a woman and a man for each of ten roles -- lead voice,
; drums, lyrics and seven more -- in each of the same ten genres. Each record's
; url is the Wikipedia page the book links it to.
;
; THE TWO TABLES. albums holds what you play: its name, artist and genre are the
; row; year, rank in its genre, summary and awards are in the record, with
; format and rating left for you. performers holds the musicians and the acts
; the albums are credited to, one record each: a musician with an album of her
; own, such as Alicia Keys, is one performer.
;
; HOW THEY CONNECT. Say Related (Shift+R) on an album names who recorded it; on
; a musician, the albums and the band. A performer "recorded" an album, and a
; musician is a "member_of" a band in the table: John Bonham of Led Zeppelin
; leads to Led Zeppelin IV.
;
; THE PICK LISTS carry the books' own words: each genre with the sentence the
; book uses for it, and each role with the opening paragraph of its chapter.
; scripts\makeMusicTrail in DbDo's development folder rebuilds this file from
; the two books.
;
; [Database] decides which table the window starts on. Each [Table:<name>]
; decides which fields the row speaks, in what order, and how the rows are
; sorted. DbDo writes these keys back whenever you change the columns, the sort
; or the filter, so what you adjust is remembered here. A row is heard, not
; seen, so the field that decides whether to keep listening comes first, and
; three fields is the target. The first field is also what first-letter
; navigation searches.

[Database]

InitialTable = albums

[Table:albums]

SelectFields = name, artist, genre
; OrderFields, 13 October 2026: the books' own order: each genre's ten greatest albums, best first.
OrderFields = genre, rank
WhereFilter =

[Table:lookups]

SelectFields = fld, val
OrderFields = fld, ordinal
WhereFilter =

[Table:maps]

SelectFields = kind, prime1, prime2
OrderFields = kind, prime1
WhereFilter =

[Table:performers]

SelectFields = name, role, genre
OrderFields = name
WhereFilter =
